<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vahka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feke Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vahka, near modern Feke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> province of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, is a castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> mountains north of the Cilician plain. It was an early stronghold of the Rubenid Armenian lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in the late 11th and 12th centuries, guarding routes between the uplands and the plain. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> took it in 1137, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> later recovered it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vahka.jpg" alt="Vahka" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeynel Cebeci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feke,_Adana_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
